lessons.body:
Firmware spends much of its time representing things: a sampled value, a count, a setting and whether an operation succeeded. C makes the representation part of the program. A **declaration** gives an object a type and a name; an **initialiser** gives it its starting value. A semicolon ends the declaration.

## Read a small complete program

This is a complete C11 program for a computer, using a target that provides the exact-width integer types. The headers make library declarations and type names available; they do not initialise a microcontroller. `main` is this hosted program's entry function. The braces enclose a function's statements.

```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <stdio.h>

bool above_limit(uint16_t sample) {
    const uint16_t limit = 300u;
    return sample > limit;
}

int main(void) {
    uint16_t reading = 320u;
    bool high = above_limit(reading);
    // Print false as 0, true as 1.
    printf("%u\n", (unsigned)high);
    return 0;
}
```

It prints `1`. The function accepts one function argument, named `sample` inside the function. `return` sends the comparison result to the caller. `bool` represents false or true; `<stdbool.h>` supplies the convenient C11 names. The `u` suffix makes a literal unsigned. The cast `(unsigned)` gives `printf` the argument type its `%u` conversion expects. A function call does not automatically share a caller's local variable: here the value is passed into the function parameter.

## Size and meaning are separate

An **unsigned integer** represents non-negative integers. `<stdint.h>` provides names such as `uint16_t` when the implementation supports the corresponding exact-width type. `uint16_t` has exactly 16 value bits and no padding; it can represent 0 through 65,535. These exact-width names are optional in C11, so check the target toolchain. Plain `int` is not guaranteed to be 32 bits.

`const` means this declaration does not permit modifying `limit` through that name. It does not promise storage in flash. A type also does not encode a measurement unit: a count in milliseconds and a count in microseconds may have the same C type. Put the unit in the name and document the range.

> [!analogy] Real-world analogy: choosing a labelled measuring jar
> A labelled jar tells you what belongs inside and how much it can hold. Choosing a bigger jar does not tell you whether its contents are flour or sugar.
>
> **How it maps:** An integer type defines a representable range; a name and contract explain what that value means. Choose both before using the value in control decisions.
>
> **Where it stops:** C does not enforce a physical unit just because a name includes it. Conversions and arithmetic still follow language rules and can lose information.

## Do not treat narrowing as validation

Converting 256 to `uint8_t`, where that type exists, produces 0 under C's unsigned conversion rules. It does not report an invalid input. A **cast** requests a conversion; it is not a range check. Check a value's allowed range before storing it in a narrower type. Signed overflow is a different case and must not be assumed to wrap.

## Key takeaways
- Declarations name a type and an object; function parameters receive argument values.
- Exact-width unsigned types make a chosen representation explicit when the target provides them.
- Keep units and valid ranges in the program's names and contracts; casts do not validate input.

## Further reading
- [C11 draft: declarations, functions and integer types](https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n1570.pdf)

A mechanical switch can make and break contact several times during a press or release. Software that treats every raw transition as an action may report several presses. **Debouncing** separates an unstable raw input from the stable state the application is allowed to use.

## State that survives each call

This example requires three consecutive matching samples. It is a teaching policy, not a claim that every real switch settles after three samples. A `struct` groups related fields. The `typedef` declaration gives the grouped type the name `Debounce`. A pointer to that structure lets the update function modify the same state on successive calls; `d->seen` accesses a field through that pointer.

This is a complete C11 host model. The update runs in one foreground context, takes bounded work per sample, and does not sleep:

```c
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <stddef.h>
#include <stdio.h>

typedef struct {
    bool candidate, stable;
    unsigned seen;
} Debounce;

bool update(Debounce *d, bool raw) {
    if (raw != d->candidate) {
        d->candidate = raw;
        d->seen = 1u;
    } else if (d->seen < 3u) {
        d->seen++;
    }
    if (d->seen == 3u &&
        d->stable != d->candidate) {
        d->stable = d->candidate;
        return true; // stable change
    }
    return false;
}

int main(void) {
    Debounce d = {false, false, 0u};
    bool raw[] = {false, true, false,
                  true, true, true};
    for (size_t i = 0; i < 6u; ++i) {
        if (update(&d, raw[i]))
            printf("%u\n", (unsigned)i);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

It prints `5`, the zero-based sample index at which the stable state changes to true. Here the raw values already represent "pressed", after active-low interpretation. The first attempted true run is interrupted by a false sample. The final three true samples form one accepted change. Further true samples produce no additional stable-change notification.

> [!analogy] Real-world analogy: waiting for a repeated answer
> When a noisy announcement keeps changing, you can wait for the same answer several times before acting on it.
>
> **How it maps:** The candidate records the latest possible answer, the count records consecutive agreement and the stable state is the accepted result. Reporting only a stable change avoids repeating the same action every sample.
>
> **Where it stops:** Agreement at sample instants does not prove the contact stayed steady between them. A real debounce interval must suit the switch, sampling schedule and how quickly the application must react.

## Sampling frequency is part of the policy

If these samples occur every 5 ms starting at time zero, the three final true samples occur at 15, 20 and 25 ms. The state changes at 25 ms. Three sample instants span two sampling intervals, not three. A missed or irregular sample changes the real elapsed time represented by the count.

The later timing module schedules calls using a timer. Do not poll this function as fast as the CPU allows and then treat its count as milliseconds. Measure the actual switch behaviour and choose a stable-time requirement. If the application wants only presses, additionally require that the new stable state is true; a release is also a stable change.

## Key takeaways
- Keep raw input, candidate state and accepted state distinct.
- Consecutive sample counts need an explicit sampling schedule.
- Emit an action on the desired stable transition rather than on every matching sample.

## Further reading
- [C11 draft: structures, pointers and iteration](https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n1570.pdf)
- [Microchip AN2805: how contact bounce creates multiple triggers](https://onlinedocs.microchip.com/oxy/GUID-7FD12F93-9B28-4EF0-B36F-3A147484BEA1-en-US-3/GUID-5B5C8194-1C66-43F0-A557-ADC76635525B.html)

A pointer can refer to the correct kind of object and still be unsafe to use. Two further questions matter: is that object still alive, and is the requested element inside its bounds? C generally leaves these checks to the program.

## A local array belongs to its scope

An ordinary local array has **automatic storage duration**. Its lifetime ends when execution leaves its enclosing block. Returning a pointer to that local array does not keep the array alive. Reading or writing the former object afterwards has undefined behaviour: the language does not promise an outcome, even if one test appears to work.

An object declared with **static storage duration** lasts for the execution of the program. That does not mean it is non-volatile storage or that its contents survive a reset. Static objects can also introduce shared state, so they are not an automatic replacement for a clear ownership contract.

> [!analogy] Real-world analogy: a key to a borrowed workbench
> Keeping the key to a borrowed workbench does not extend your booking. You need both the key and permission to use the bench at that time.
>
> **How it maps:** A pointer alone does not extend an object's lifetime. The caller must know who owns the storage and how long it remains available.
>
> **Where it stops:** C does not check the booking for every access. A stale pointer can cause undefined behaviour rather than a helpful refusal.

## Let the caller provide the storage

This complete C11 host example writes three readings into an array supplied by its caller. `size_t` is an unsigned type suitable for object sizes and array counts. `NULL` is a null pointer constant; the function rejects it here before dereferencing.

```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stddef.h>
#include <stdio.h>

size_t fill(uint16_t *out, size_t cap) {
    if (out == NULL || cap < 3u)
        return 0u;
    out[0] = 10u;
    out[1] = 20u;
    out[2] = 30u;
    return 3u;
}

int main(void) {
    uint16_t samples[3] = {0u};
    size_t count = fill(samples, 3u);
    printf("%u %u\n", (unsigned)count,
           (unsigned)samples[2]);
    return 0;
}
```

It prints `3 30`. The array's valid indexes are 0, 1 and 2. Index 3 is one past its end and cannot be dereferenced. Passing an array to this function supplies a pointer to its first element, not a copy of the whole array or its capacity. The separate `cap` parameter records the caller's promise about writable elements.

The check is useful but cannot discover whether that promise is true: a non-null pointer and a large claimed capacity do not prove a valid buffer exists. The caller must supply a live writable array of at least that capacity. In this example the caller's own array remains alive for the whole call and subsequent print.

## Prefer explicit ownership before allocation tricks

For a small controller, fixed buffers with documented owners often make memory use easier to reason about. They still require bounds checks. Dynamic allocation has additional ownership and failure handling, but it is not needed for this beginner example. Decide who may change each buffer, when it may be reused and whether another context can still be using it.

## Key takeaways
- A pointer does not extend the lifetime of its object.
- Pass buffer capacity and return how much valid data was produced.
- Bounds checks depend on an honest caller contract as well as checks on each array index.

## Further reading
- [C11 draft: object lifetime, arrays and pointer arithmetic](https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n1570.pdf)
